1. Use Google to search public social posts
Google often finds public Instagram and Facebook posts better than Instagram’s own web search.
Use searches like these:
site:instagram.com "St. Louis" "looking for" "trivia"
site:instagram.com "STL" "trivia night" "looking for"
site:facebook.com/groups "St. Louis" "looking for someone to host trivia"
site:facebook.com "St. Louis" "karaoke" "trivia" "Tuesdays"
site:instagram.com "St. Louis" "new weekly event"
site:instagram.com "St. Louis" "slow night" restaurant OR bar
Reference this list of area + search term combos.
"St. Louis" "looking for someone to host trivia"
"St. Louis" "looking for trivia host"
"STL" "looking for trivia host"
"St. Louis" "someone to host trivia"
"St. Louis" "host trivia night"
"St. Louis" "trivia company"
"St. Louis" "karaoke and trivia" "Tuesday"
"St. Louis" "weekly events" "bar"
"St. Louis" "new weekly event" "restaurant"
"St. Louis" "Tuesday night" "bar" "trivia"
"St. Louis" "Wednesday night" "bar" "trivia"

2. Search Facebook groups.
For this specific business, Facebook is probably better than Instagram for intent. People ask for recommendations, vendors, hosts, venues, and event ideas in Facebook groups.
Search inside local groups for:
trivia
trivia host
host trivia
karaoke trivia
Tuesday night
slow night
bar events
restaurant events
Groups to check:
St. Louis restaurant groups
St. Louis small business groups
St. Louis bartender/server groups
Neighborhood groups: Soulard, Dogtown, The Grove, Tower Grove, South City, Central West End, Maplewood, Kirkwood, etc.
Local events groups
“What’s going on in St. Louis” type groups
A useful real-world signal: public Facebook group results often include people asking about trivia nights, venues, or hosts. I found search results with posts like people asking for trivia-night recommendations, nonprofit trivia venues, and someone looking for karaoke/trivia hosting downtown.

4. Instagram: search hashtags, not just accounts
Instagram is weaker for text search, especially on desktop. Use hashtags and venue discovery.
Search these hashtags:
#stltrivia
#stlouistrivia
#trivianightstl
#stlouisbar
#stlbars
#stlrestaurants
#stlnightlife
#stlevents
#southcitystl
#soulard
#thegrovestl
#towergrove
#maplewoodmo
#dogtownstl
#centralwestend
You are looking for venues posting things like:
“New weekly event”
“Trivia night coming soon”
“What events should we add?”
“Looking for something fun on Tuesdays”
“Come out for karaoke/bingo/open mic”
“Now booking events”
Venues already doing karaoke, bingo, open mic, music bingo, or board game nights are good prospects because they already understand weekly entertainment. Public Instagram results show lots of venues promoting weekly trivia, which is useful for competitor mapping and for identifying nearby venues that may be receptive to similar programming.

5. Search for “almost trivia” venues
A venue does not need to say “we want trivia” to be a good lead.
Better lead signals:
Signal
Why it matters
Karaoke night
Already uses hosted entertainment
Bingo/music bingo
Similar weekly structure
Open mic
Has slow-night programming
New ownership
Often testing events
“New happy hour”
Trying to build traffic
“Tuesday specials”
Tuesday is often slow
“Wednesday specials”
Another good trivia night
“Private events”
They may already value event programming
“Game night”
Closest behavioral overlap

Search phrases:
"St. Louis" "karaoke night" "Tuesday"
"St. Louis" "music bingo"
"St. Louis" "game night" bar
"St. Louis" "new ownership" bar
"St. Louis" "Tuesday specials" bar
"St. Louis" "weekly events" restaurant
This may produce more usable leads than searching only for “want trivia,” because most venues do not publicly announce that they are shopping for a trivia vendor.

6. Replicate social listening tools.
Tools like Hootsuite and HubSpot describe social monitoring features for tracking mentions, keywords, competitors, and social conversations across networks. Hootsuite advertises cross-network social search/monitoring, and HubSpot’s 2026 guide describes monitoring mentions, reposts, competitors, and some keyword streams depending on account level.
We are going to build something similar. First build a keyword list, then use a tool to automate it.
